<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Edward</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Edward is a lake in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albertine-rift">Albertine Rift</a> on the border between Uganda and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>. Linked by the Kazinga Channel to Lake George, it drains northwards through the Semliki River into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-albert">Lake Albert</a>. The kingdom of Ankole grew up in the grasslands to its east. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morton-stanley">Henry Morton Stanley</a> named it in 1888 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>, the future Edward VII.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-edward.jpg" alt="Lake Edward" width="320" height="323" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: European Union, Sentinel-2 Imagery · Attribution · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_edward_sentinel-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lake Edward, locally Rwitanzigye or Rweru, is one of the smaller African Great Lakes. It lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albertine-rift">Albertine Rift</a>, the western branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-african-rift">East African Rift</a>, with its northern shore a few kilometres south of the equator. At an elevation of 920 metres it is 77 kilometres long and 40 kilometres wide at its maximum points, and covers 2,325 square kilometres. About two thirds of its waters are in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a> and one third in Uganda. The Nyamugasani, Ishasha, Rutshuru, Ntungwe and Rwindi rivers feed it, and Lake George empties into it by the Kazinga Channel.</p><p>The western escarpment of the Rift Valley towers up to 2,000 metres above the western shore, while the southern and eastern shores are flat lava plains. The Semliki River carries its water north into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-albert">Lake Albert</a>, whence it joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Welsh explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morton-stanley">Henry Morton Stanley</a> first saw the lake in 1888, during the Emin Pasha Relief Expedition, and it was named in honour of Albert Edward, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> and later King Edward VII. In 1973 Uganda and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaire">Zaire</a> renamed it Lake Idi Amin, after the Ugandan ruler; it recovered its former name after his overthrow in 1979.</p><p>The region shows much evidence of volcanic activity over the last 5,000 years. The Katwe-Kikorongo and Bunyaruguru volcanic fields, with extensive cones and craters, lie either side of the Kazinga Channel on the north-western shore. It is thought that Lakes George and Edward were once one larger lake, divided by lava from these fields, with the Kazinga Channel as the remnant of the union. Stanley visited the crater lake of Katwe in 1889 and noted its depression, its salinity and a nearby sulphurous spring, but did not connect them to volcanism; the volcanic origin of the area was first reported by G. F. Scott Elliot in 1894.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The lake lies wholly within Virunga National Park in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a> and Queen Elizabeth National Park in Uganda, and has little settlement on its shores. Ishango in the north holds a park ranger training facility; the main Congolese settlement in the south is Vitshumbi, and the Ugandan settlements are Mweya and Katwe. Lake Katwe is the chief producer of salt for Uganda. Its fish include Bagrus docmak, two species of Oreochromis tilapia and over 50 haplochromine species, and fishing is important to local residents.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lake Edward” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Nkore, known as Ankole from the colonial period, grew up in the grasslands of south-western Uganda between <b>Lake Edward</b> and the Kagera River.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ankole">Kingdom of Ankole · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
